/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccst-networking.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccst-networking", [["cs1",0,1,"A web page request moves down the stack on a PC. At which TCP/IP layer are TCP port numbers added?",["Application","Transport","Internet","Network access"],1,"TCP and UDP port numbers belong to the Transport layer. The Internet layer adds IP addresses, not ports.","Objective 1.1",["The Application layer creates the request itself (HTTP, DNS); it does not add port numbers to a segment.",null,"The Internet layer adds source and destination IP addresses, not TCP or UDP ports.","The Network access layer adds MAC addresses and turns frames into signals; ports are already in place by then."]],["cs2",0,1,"Which address does a switch use to forward a frame within the local network?",["The destination IP address","The destination port number","The destination MAC address","The default gateway address"],2,"Switches forward Ethernet frames by destination MAC address. IP addresses are used by routers to move packets between networks.","Objective 1.1",["Routers use destination IP addresses to move packets between networks; a Layer 2 switch looks only at the frame's MAC header.","Port numbers identify applications at the Transport layer, and a basic switch does not read them to forward frames.",null,"The default gateway address is what a host uses to reach other networks; a switch does not forward frames based on it."]],["cs3",0,1,"What is the unit of data called at the data link layer?",["Frame","Segment","Packet","Bit"],0,"Layer 2 encapsulates data in frames. Packets are Layer 3 and segments are Layer 4.","Objective 1.1",[null,"A segment is the Layer 4 (transport) unit that carries TCP or UDP port numbers.","A packet is the Layer 3 unit that carries IP addresses, one layer above frames.","Bits are the Layer 1 physical-layer representation, not the data link unit."]],["cs4",0,1,"A customer pays for a 1 Gbps internet plan, but a speed test over Wi-Fi shows 380 Mbps. What does the 380 Mbps figure describe?",["Bandwidth","Round-trip latency","Jitter","Throughput"],3,"Throughput is the data rate actually achieved. Bandwidth is the 1 Gbps maximum capacity of the plan.","Objective 1.2",["Bandwidth is the maximum capacity of the link, which here is the 1 Gbps the customer pays for.","Latency is a delay measured in milliseconds, not a data rate in Mbps.","Jitter is the variation in delay between packets, measured in milliseconds, not a transfer rate.",null]],["cs5",0,1,"A technician wants to measure the real data rate between two office PCs without using the internet. Which tool fits best?",["iperf","A web speed test","nslookup","tracert"],0,"iperf sends test traffic between a client and server that you control, so it measures the local path. A web speed test measures the path to an internet server instead.","Objective 1.2",[null,"A web speed test measures the path to an internet server, which is exactly what the technician wants to avoid.","nslookup queries DNS to resolve names; it does not measure data rates.","tracert lists the router hops and their delays along a path; it does not measure throughput."]],["cs6",0,1,"A user on a video call says voices arrive late but clear. Which measurement describes how long data takes to travel from sender to receiver?",["Bandwidth","Latency","Throughput","Packet size"],1,"Latency is the delay for data to cross the network. Bandwidth is capacity and does not by itself describe delay.","Objective 1.2",["Bandwidth is the capacity of the link; plenty of bandwidth can still arrive with a long delay.",null,"Throughput is the actual data rate achieved, which describes how much gets through, not how long it takes.","Packet size affects efficiency slightly but is not a measurement of travel time across the network."]],["cs7",0,1,"A university connects the networks in its library, dorms and science buildings on one campus. What type of network is this?",["PAN","WAN","CAN","MAN"],2,"A campus area network links buildings on one site. A MAN covers a city, and a WAN spans larger distances.","Objective 1.3",["A PAN covers a few meters around one person, such as Bluetooth devices, not a whole campus.","A WAN spans long distances such as cities or countries, usually through a service provider, not buildings on one site.",null,"A MAN covers a metropolitan area such as a city, which is larger than a single campus."]],["cs8",0,1,"Wireless earbuds are paired with a phone using Bluetooth. What type of network is this?",["PAN","WLAN","LAN","CAN"],0,"A personal area network covers a few meters around one person. A WLAN is Wi-Fi covering a building or home.","Objective 1.3",[null,"A WLAN is a Wi-Fi network covering a home or building, not a short-range Bluetooth link.","A LAN connects devices across a building or home, usually with Ethernet and switches, not a phone-to-earbud pairing.","A CAN links several buildings on one campus, far larger than a personal Bluetooth link."]],["cs9",0,1,"A company links its offices in three different countries through a service provider. What type of network is this?",["MAN","LAN","CAN","WAN"],3,"A wide area network connects sites over long distances. A MAN is limited to about one city.","Objective 1.3",["A MAN covers about one city, not offices in three countries.","A LAN covers a single building or site, not links between countries.","A CAN connects buildings on one campus, not sites in different countries.",null]],["cs10",0,1,"A small business wants email and documents without managing any servers or operating systems. Which cloud model fits?",["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],2,"With SaaS the provider runs the whole application. IaaS still requires the customer to manage operating systems.","Objective 1.4",["IaaS provides virtual servers, so the business would still install and manage operating systems.","PaaS still requires the customer to build and manage their own applications on the platform.",null,"On-premises means the business hosts and manages its own servers, the opposite of what it wants."]],["cs11",0,1,"A company keeps its payroll servers in its own data center but runs its website on a public cloud provider. Which deployment is this?",["Hybrid cloud","Private cloud","Public cloud only","Community cloud"],0,"Combining on-premises or private resources with public cloud is a hybrid deployment. A private cloud alone would not include the public provider.","Objective 1.4",[null,"A private cloud is infrastructure dedicated to one organization; it would not include the public provider hosting the website.","Public cloud only would mean the payroll servers are also in the public cloud, but they stay in the company data center.","A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common needs, which does not describe this setup."]],["cs12",0,1,"A developer rents virtual machines and installs and patches the operating system on each one. Which service model is being used?",["SaaS","IaaS","PaaS","FaaS"],1,"IaaS provides virtual servers, storage and networking, and the customer manages the OS. PaaS would hide the OS from the developer.","Objective 1.4",["With SaaS the provider runs the whole application, so the customer never installs or patches an OS.",null,"PaaS hides the operating system; the developer deploys code and the provider patches the OS.","FaaS (serverless) runs individual functions with no servers or OS for the customer to manage."]],["cs13",0,1,"Which statement about TCP is correct?",["It sends data without any acknowledgments","It is the protocol DHCP relies on to lease out IP addresses","It has no way to detect lost data","It sets up a connection with a three-way handshake"],3,"TCP is connection-oriented: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, then acknowledged and retransmitted data. DHCP uses UDP, not TCP.","Objective 1.5",["TCP acknowledges received data; sending without acknowledgments describes UDP.","DHCP uses UDP ports 67 and 68, not TCP.","TCP uses sequence numbers and acknowledgments to detect lost data and retransmit it.",null]],["cs14",0,1,"A firewall must allow secure web browsing. Which port should be open?",["TCP 443","TCP 8080","UDP 69","TCP 21"],0,"HTTPS uses TCP 443. TCP 8080 is a common alternate port for plain, unencrypted HTTP.","Objective 1.5",[null,"TCP 8080 is a common alternate port for plain HTTP or proxies, not the standard port for secure browsing.","UDP 69 is TFTP, a simple file transfer protocol with no web use.","TCP 21 is the FTP control channel, not HTTPS."]],["cs15",0,1,"PCs on a network are not getting their time synchronized from the time server. Which protocol and port should be checked?",["SNMP on UDP 161","DNS on UDP 53","NTP on UDP 123","TFTP on UDP 69"],2,"Network Time Protocol uses UDP 123. DNS on 53 resolves names and does not set clocks.","Objective 1.5",["SNMP on UDP 161 is used to monitor and manage devices, not to synchronize clocks.","DNS on UDP 53 resolves names to IP addresses and does not set the time.",null,"TFTP on UDP 69 transfers files such as configs and images, not time data."]],["cs16",0,1,"A technician needs to copy a switch configuration to a simple server that has no login or authentication. Which protocol is commonly used?",["SFTP","HTTPS","SSH","TFTP"],3,"TFTP (UDP 69) is a simple transfer protocol with no authentication, often used for device configs and images. SFTP requires a login.","Objective 1.5",["SFTP runs over SSH and requires a login, so it does not match a server with no authentication.","HTTPS is for encrypted web traffic and normally requires a web server with some login for uploads.","SSH is an encrypted remote-login protocol that requires authentication.",null]],["cs17",0,1,"Which protocol does the ping command use?",["ICMP","UDP","ARP","DHCP"],0,"Ping sends ICMP echo requests and waits for echo replies. ARP resolves IP addresses to MAC addresses and is not what ping uses.","Objective 1.5",[null,"UDP is a transport protocol with ports; ping works at the Internet layer with ICMP echo messages, not UDP.","ARP maps IP addresses to MAC addresses on the local link; it may run before a ping but is not what ping sends.","DHCP assigns IP addresses to hosts and has nothing to do with testing reachability."]],["cs18",0,1,"Which pair of protocols normally uses UDP rather than TCP?",["HTTP and HTTPS","DNS queries and DHCP","SSH and FTP","SMTP and HTTPS"],1,"Standard DNS queries (UDP 53) and DHCP (UDP 67/68) use UDP. SSH and FTP use TCP.","Objective 1.5",["HTTP and HTTPS normally run over TCP (80 and 443) because web pages need reliable delivery.",null,"SSH (22) and FTP (20/21) both use TCP for reliable sessions.","SMTP (25) and HTTPS (443) both use TCP."]],["cs19",0,2,"Which of these addresses is a private IPv4 address?",["172.32.4.10","192.169.1.5","11.0.0.8","172.20.15.3"],3,"The private range 172.16.0.0/12 covers 172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255, so 172.20.15.3 is private. 172.32.4.10 is just outside that range.","Objective 2.1",["172.32.4.10 is just past the end of 172.16.0.0/12, which stops at 172.31.255.255, so it is public.","192.169.1.5 is outside 192.168.0.0/16; only 192.168.x.x is private.","11.0.0.8 is outside the 10.0.0.0/8 private range, so it is a public address.",null]],["cs20",0,2,"Twenty devices in a home all reach the internet through one public IPv4 address. What makes this possible?",["PAT (NAT overload)","DNS","DHCP relay (IP helper)","VLAN tagging"],0,"Port address translation maps many private addresses to one public address using different port numbers. DHCP hands out addresses but does not translate them.","Objective 2.1",[null,"DNS translates names into IP addresses; it does not let many devices share one public address.","DHCP relay forwards DHCP requests to a server on another subnet; it does not translate addresses.","VLAN tagging separates traffic into virtual LANs on a switch and has nothing to do with sharing a public IP."]],["cs21",0,2,"A laptop shows the address 169.254.40.12 and cannot reach anything. What is the most likely cause?",["The DNS server is down","The laptop has a duplicate IP address","The laptop did not get a reply from a DHCP server","The default gateway is configured with the wrong address"],2,"169.254.x.x is an APIPA address a device assigns itself when DHCP fails. A DNS problem would not change the IP address.","Objective 2.1",["A DNS outage breaks name resolution but does not cause the laptop to give itself a 169.254.x.x address.","A duplicate IP address causes conflicts, but a DHCP-assigned address would still be in the normal range, not APIPA.",null,"A wrong gateway stops traffic to other networks, but the laptop would still have its normal DHCP address."]],["cs22",0,2,"Which subnet mask matches the prefix /26?",["255.255.255.0","255.255.255.192","255.255.255.128","255.255.255.224"],1,"/26 is 24 bits plus 2 more: 128 + 64 = 192. 255.255.255.224 is /27.","Objective 2.2",["255.255.255.0 is /24, two bits shorter than /26.",null,"255.255.255.128 is /25, one bit shorter than /26.","255.255.255.224 is /27, one bit longer than /26."]],["cs23",0,2,"How many usable host addresses are in a /27 subnet?",["30","32","62","14"],0,"/27 leaves 5 host bits: 2^5 = 32, minus the network and broadcast addresses = 30. 32 is the total, not usable, count.","Objective 2.2",[null,"32 is the total number of addresses in a /27, including the network and broadcast addresses, which cannot be assigned.","62 usable hosts belongs to a /26, which has 6 host bits.","14 usable hosts belongs to a /28, which has 4 host bits."]],["cs24",0,2,"A host is 10.1.1.70/26. What is its network address?",["10.1.1.0","10.1.1.32","10.1.1.64","10.1.1.70"],2,"/26 uses blocks of 64 (0, 64, 128, 192). 70 falls in 64–127, so the network is 10.1.1.64.","Objective 2.2",["10.1.1.0 is the network for hosts .0 to .63; .70 is in the next /26 block.","Blocks of 32 belong to a /27; a /26 uses blocks of 64, so .32 is not a /26 network boundary.",null,"10.1.1.70 is the host's own address, not the network address, which has all host bits set to 0."]],["cs25",0,2,"PC A is 192.168.5.20/25 and PC B is 192.168.5.140/25 with no router between them. Why can't they ping each other?",["They are in different subnets","They share the same broadcast address","A /25 allows only two hosts","Both addresses are public"],0,"A /25 splits the network at .128: .0–.127 and .128–.255, so the PCs are in different subnets and need a router. They do not share a broadcast address.","Objective 2.2",[null,"They do not share a broadcast address: PC A's is .127 and PC B's is .255, which is part of why they are in different subnets.","A /25 has 7 host bits and allows 126 usable hosts; two hosts is a /30.","Both addresses are in 192.168.0.0/16, which is a private range."]],["cs26",0,2,"What is the broadcast address of 192.168.8.0/24?",["192.168.8.0","192.168.8.1","192.168.255.255","192.168.8.255"],3,"The broadcast address has all host bits set to 1, which is .255 in a /24. 192.168.8.0 is the network address.","Objective 2.2",["192.168.8.0 is the network address, with all host bits set to 0.","192.168.8.1 is the first usable host address, often a gateway, not the broadcast.","192.168.255.255 would be the broadcast for 192.168.0.0/16, not for a /24.",null]],["cs27",0,2,"Which is a valid shortened form of 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0010?",["2001:db8::1","2001:db8::10","2001:db8:0:10","2001::db8::10"],1,"Remove leading zeros and replace the zero run with :: once, giving 2001:db8::10. The :: can appear only once, so 2001::db8::10 is invalid.","Objective 2.3",["2001:db8::1 ends in 1, but the original last hextet is 0010, which shortens to 10, not 1.",null,"2001:db8:0:10 has only four hextets and no ::, so it does not represent the full eight-hextet address.","The :: shortcut can be used only once; two of them make the address ambiguous and invalid."]],["cs28",0,2,"An IPv6 address begins with fe80. What type of address is it?",["Global unicast","Multicast","Link-local","Loopback"],2,"fe80::/10 is link-local, used only on the local link. Multicast addresses start with ff.","Objective 2.3",["Global unicast addresses are in 2000::/3 and usually start with 2 or 3, not fe80.","Multicast addresses start with ff, not fe80.",null,"The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, not an address starting with fe80."]],["cs29",0,2,"A laptop builds its own IPv6 global address from the router's advertised prefix without a DHCP server. What is this called?",["SLAAC","APIPA","PAT","DHCPv4"],0,"Stateless address autoconfiguration uses the prefix from router advertisements. APIPA is an IPv4 fallback, not IPv6.","Objective 2.3",[null,"APIPA is the IPv4 169.254.x.x fallback when DHCP fails; it does not use router-advertised prefixes.","PAT translates many private addresses to one public address and does not assign addresses to hosts.","DHCPv4 is a server-based IPv4 protocol, while the laptop here builds an IPv6 address without a server."]],["cs30",0,3,"A technician must run a network cable 70 meters across an office ceiling and support 10 Gbps. Which cable is the best choice?",["Cat 5e","Cat 6a","Cat 3","RG-6 coax"],1,"Cat 6a supports 10 Gbps up to 100 m. Cat 5e is rated for 1 Gbps.","Objective 3.1",["Cat 5e is rated for 1 Gbps; it is not specified for 10GBASE-T over 70 m.",null,"Cat 3 is old voice-grade cable that supports only about 10 Mbps Ethernet.","RG-6 coax is used for cable TV and cable modems, not for 10 Gbps Ethernet runs to office devices."]],["cs31",0,3,"Which connector is used on a standard Ethernet patch cable?",["RJ-11","BNC","F-type","RJ-45"],3,"RJ-45 is the 8-pin connector used for twisted-pair Ethernet. RJ-11 is the smaller telephone connector.","Objective 3.1",["RJ-11 is the smaller 6-position connector used for telephone and DSL lines.","BNC is a bayonet coax connector used on old Ethernet and video gear, not modern patch cables.","F-type is a threaded coax connector for cable TV and cable modems.",null]],["cs32",0,3,"Two buildings are 5 km apart. Which cable should connect them?",["Single-mode fiber","Multimode fiber","Cat 6 shielded copper cable","Coaxial cable"],0,"Single-mode fiber carries signals for many kilometers. Multimode is limited to much shorter runs, and copper Ethernet stops at 100 m.","Objective 3.1",[null,"Multimode fiber is designed for shorter runs, typically hundreds of meters, not 5 km.","Copper Ethernet, shielded or not, is limited to 100 m per run.","Coaxial cable is not used for Ethernet links of this length between buildings and would not reach 5 km at modern speeds."]],["cs33",0,3,"A cable modem connects to the provider's wall outlet with a threaded, screw-on connector. Which connector is it?",["LC","RJ-45","F-type","ST"],2,"Cable TV and cable internet use coax with F-type screw-on connectors. LC and ST are fiber connectors.","Objective 3.1",["LC is a small latching fiber connector, not a threaded coax connector.","RJ-45 is a clip-in twisted-pair Ethernet connector, not a screw-on coax connector.",null,"ST is a round bayonet fiber connector, not used for cable TV coax."]],["cs34",0,3,"A switch has an empty slot that accepts a hot-swappable module so it can connect to fiber. What is this module called?",["PoE injector","SFP transceiver","Patch panel","Media keystone"],1,"SFP (small form-factor pluggable) transceivers plug into switch slots to support fiber or copper. A patch panel is a passive termination point, not a module.","Objective 3.1",["A PoE injector adds power to an Ethernet cable; it is an external box, not a module that adds fiber support.",null,"A patch panel is a passive rack termination point for cable runs, not a pluggable switch module.","A keystone is a snap-in jack for wall plates or panels; it does not plug into a switch slot."]],["cs35",0,3,"Which fiber connector is small, square and commonly found in pairs on SFP modules?",["BNC","RJ-11","F-type","LC"],3,"LC is the small form-factor fiber connector common on SFPs. BNC and F-type are coax connectors.","Objective 3.1",["BNC is a round bayonet coaxial connector, not a fiber connector.","RJ-11 is a copper telephone connector, not fiber.","F-type is a threaded coax connector for cable TV and modems.",null]],["cs36",0,3,"Users near a break room lose 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi each time the microwave oven runs. What is the best fix?",["Move those users to the 5 GHz band","Lower the AP's channel to 1","Switch the network to WEP","Increase the DHCP lease time for those laptops"],0,"Microwave ovens interfere in the 2.4 GHz band; 5 GHz avoids that interference. Changing security settings does nothing for radio interference.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Channel 1 is still in the 2.4 GHz band, and microwave ovens interfere across much of that band, so the drops would continue.","WEP is a broken security setting and has no effect on radio interference.","DHCP lease time controls how long an address is kept; it cannot stop the radio signal being drowned out."]],["cs37",0,3,"Which statement correctly compares cellular and Wi-Fi networks?",["Wi-Fi uses licensed spectrum and cellular does not","Both require an Ethernet cable to the device","Cellular uses licensed spectrum run by a carrier","Cellular is limited to a single building"],2,"Cellular (4G/5G) runs on spectrum licensed to carriers. Wi-Fi uses unlicensed bands, so the first option reverses them.","Objective 3.2",["This reverses the facts: Wi-Fi uses unlicensed bands and cellular uses licensed spectrum.","Neither needs an Ethernet cable to the device; both are wireless technologies.",null,"Cellular networks cover wide areas through carrier towers; it is Wi-Fi that is typically limited to a building."]],["cs38",0,3,"Which three 2.4 GHz channels do not overlap in North America?",["1, 6 and 11","2, 7 and 12","1, 5 and 9","3, 8 and 13"],0,"Channels 1, 6 and 11 are the standard non-overlapping 2.4 GHz set in North America. Other combinations overlap.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Channels 2, 7 and 12 are 5 apart like 1/6/11 but are not the standard set, and channel 12 is not normally allowed for Wi-Fi in North America.","Channels 1, 5 and 9 are only 4 channels apart, so their 20 MHz signals overlap.","Channel 13 is not normally used in North America, and 3/8/13 is not the standard non-overlapping plan."]],["cs39",0,3,"A visitor needs to join the office guest Wi-Fi using WPA2-Personal. What must they know?",["The AP's MAC address and serial number","The SSID and the passphrase","The switch VLAN ID and gateway","Their username and a smart card"],1,"WPA2-Personal requires the network name (SSID) and the pre-shared passphrase. Username-based logins are for WPA2-Enterprise.","Objective 3.2",["The AP's MAC address and serial number identify the hardware; a client does not need them to join.",null,"VLAN IDs and gateways are assigned by the network after joining; the visitor does not enter them.","A username and smart card are credentials for WPA2-Enterprise with 802.1X, not WPA2-Personal."]],["cs40",0,3,"A copper cable run between a switch and a PC is 130 meters long and the link is slow with many errors. What is the problem?",["The cable is too short","The PC needs a static IP","The run exceeds the 100 m Ethernet limit","The switch needs an SFP module"],2,"Twisted-pair Ethernet is limited to 100 m; beyond that attenuation causes errors. The IP configuration has nothing to do with signal loss.","Objective 3.2",["The cable is too long, not too short; short cables are not a cause of errors on Ethernet.","Signal loss on a long cable is a physical-layer issue; a static IP would not change it.",null,"An SFP module is used for fiber or special copper uplinks; the existing copper run is simply too long."]],["cs41",0,3,"A smart thermostat, a door sensor and a security camera are all on the network. What category of endpoints are these?",["Servers","Thin clients","Workstations","IoT devices"],3,"Internet of Things devices are single-purpose, connected sensors and appliances. Servers provide services to many clients.","Objective 3.3",["Servers provide services such as files or email to many clients, unlike single-purpose sensors.","Thin clients are low-power computers that run a remote desktop, not sensors and appliances.","Workstations are full user computers such as desktops, not single-purpose connected devices.",null]],["cs42",0,3,"On a Windows PC, which command shows the IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers and MAC address?",["ipconfig /all","netstat -an","ping -t","tracert -d 8.8.8.8"],0,"ipconfig /all shows the full adapter configuration. Plain ipconfig leaves out DNS and MAC details, and netstat shows connections.","Objective 3.4",[null,"netstat -an lists active connections and listening ports, not the adapter's address settings.","ping -t pings a host continuously; it tests reachability and does not show configuration.","tracert -d traces the route to a host without name lookups; it does not display local adapter settings."]],["cs43",0,3,"A Linux user wants to see the addresses assigned to each interface. Which modern command should they run?",["ipconfig /all","ip addr","show ip interface brief","nslookup"],1,"ip addr (ip a) lists interfaces and addresses on Linux. ipconfig is the Windows command.","Objective 3.4",["ipconfig /all is a Windows command and does not exist on Linux.",null,"show ip interface brief is a Cisco IOS command, not a Linux command.","nslookup queries DNS for name resolution; it does not list interface addresses."]],["cs44",0,3,"An iPhone user says Wi-Fi shows connected but apps do not load. Where can the technician check the phone's IP address and router address?",["The App Store","The phone's Bluetooth settings","The Wi-Fi settings, info icon next to the network","The Battery settings"],2,"On iOS, tapping the info (i) icon beside the joined Wi-Fi network shows the IP address, subnet mask and router. Bluetooth settings do not show IP details.","Objective 3.4",["The App Store installs apps and has no network configuration details.","Bluetooth settings manage paired devices and do not show Wi-Fi IP settings.",null,"Battery settings show power usage, not network addresses."]],["cs45",0,4,"An engineer asks what a switch port LED is doing, and the technician reports solid green that flickers when a PC is used. What does this usually mean?",["The port is disabled","The port has a link and is passing traffic","The port has failed POST","The port is blocked by Spanning Tree Protocol"],1,"Green means link up, and blinking green shows activity. Amber usually indicates blocked, disabled or faulty.","Objective 4.1",["A disabled port usually shows no light or amber, not green with activity.",null,"A failed POST shows up on the system LED, typically amber, rather than a green port with activity.","A port blocked by STP shows amber, not green."]],["cs46",0,4,"A newly connected switch port stays amber for a long time, while other ports turn green. What should the technician report to the engineer?",["The port is working normally at full speed","The PoE budget has increased","The port may be blocked, disabled or faulty","The switch firmware is up to date"],2,"A steady amber port light on a Cisco switch commonly means the port is blocked (for example by STP), disabled or has a fault. Green would indicate normal link.","Objective 4.1",["A port working normally shows green, not a long-lasting amber.","Amber port lights do not indicate the PoE budget, and PoE budgets do not increase on their own.",null,"Port LEDs say nothing about firmware versions."]],["cs47",0,4,"The system LED on a Cisco switch is off and none of the port lights are on. What should the technician check first?",["The DNS server","The VLAN configuration","The DHCP scope","The power supply and power cable"],3,"If the system light is off the switch likely has no power. VLAN and DHCP settings cannot affect the system LED.","Objective 4.1",["A DNS server problem cannot turn off the switch's system and port LEDs.","VLAN settings change which ports share a broadcast domain but do not turn off the LEDs.","A DHCP scope affects addresses handed to clients, not the switch's own lights.",null]],["cs48",0,4,"An engineer sends a diagram showing SW1 Gi1/0/24 connecting to R1 G0/0. What should the technician do?",["Connect a patch cable from port Gi1/0/24 on SW1 to port G0/0 on R1","Connect any free switch port to the router's console port","Connect SW1 port 1 to R1 port 24","Connect R1's USB port to the first free SFP slot on SW1 using a console cable"],0,"Diagrams list the exact device and interface at each end of a link, so the cable goes from SW1 Gi1/0/24 to R1 G0/0. Connecting any free port would not match the design.","Objective 4.2",[null,"The console port is for management, and the diagram calls for a specific data link between named interfaces.","This swaps the port numbers; the diagram names Gi1/0/24 on SW1 and G0/0 on R1.","A console cable is for CLI management and cannot create a routed data link into an SFP slot."]],["cs49",0,4,"After patching a cable from the diagram, the technician wants to confirm it is in the right port without logging in. What is the quickest check?",["Reboot the switch","Look for the link light on the expected port turning green","Replace the patch panel","Change the port's VLAN"],1,"The port LED shows link as soon as the other end is connected, confirming the physical connection. Rebooting disrupts users and proves nothing.","Objective 4.2",["Rebooting the switch disrupts every user and does not show which port the cable is in.",null,"Replacing the patch panel is a major change and does nothing to confirm the new connection.","Changing the VLAN is a configuration change that could break the port; it does not verify the physical patch."]],["cs50",0,4,"Which port is used to manage a new Cisco router that has no IP address configured yet?",["An SFP port","A PoE port","The console port","A serial WAN port"],2,"The console port gives out-of-band CLI access with no IP needed. Serial ports connect to WAN links, not management terminals.","Objective 4.3",["An SFP port is a data port for fiber or copper modules and needs IP configuration to be used for management.","A PoE port is a data port that also supplies power; it is not an out-of-band management port.",null,"A serial port connects to WAN circuits, not to a technician's terminal."]],["cs51",0,4,"A VoIP phone has no power adapter but lights up once plugged into the switch. What feature is the switch port providing?",["Power over Ethernet","Port security","Link aggregation","VLAN trunking"],0,"PoE delivers power over the Ethernet cable to phones, APs and cameras. Trunking carries VLANs but no power.","Objective 4.3",[null,"Port security limits which MAC addresses can use a port; it supplies no power.","Link aggregation bundles several links for more bandwidth; it does not power devices.","Trunking carries multiple VLANs over one link but does not deliver power."]],["cs52",0,4,"Which cable connects a laptop's USB port to the RJ-45 console port on a Cisco switch?",["A Cat 6 crossover cable","A coax jumper","A single-mode fiber patch","A USB-to-RJ-45 console (rollover) cable"],3,"Console access uses a rollover-style console cable, often USB to RJ-45. A crossover is for Ethernet links, not console access.","Objective 4.3",["A crossover cable connects Ethernet devices directly; it does not provide a serial console connection.","Coax jumpers are for cable TV and modems, not Cisco console ports.","Single-mode fiber carries data over long distances and has no console function.",null]],["cs53",0,4,"A PC can reach other PCs on its subnet but nothing on other networks. Its IP and mask are correct. What is the most likely missing setting?",["DNS suffix","Default gateway","MAC address","Proxy server port number"],1,"The default gateway is where a host sends traffic for remote networks. A missing DNS setting would still allow pinging remote IP addresses.","Objective 4.4",["A missing DNS suffix affects name completion, but the PC could still reach remote networks by IP.",null,"Every network card already has a burned-in MAC address; it is not a setting the user configures.","A proxy setting affects only web traffic through a proxy and would not block all remote networks."]],["cs54",0,4,"PC 10.0.5.10/24 sends a packet to 10.0.9.20. Where does the PC send the frame?",["Directly to 10.0.9.20's MAC address","To the DNS server","To every host as a broadcast","To its default gateway's MAC address"],3,"10.0.9.20 is outside 10.0.5.0/24, so the PC sends the frame to its gateway, which routes it. It cannot reach the remote host's MAC directly.","Objective 4.4",["The destination is on a different network, so the PC cannot ARP for or reach its MAC directly.","The DNS server resolves names; packets to an IP address are not sent through it.","Unicast traffic to a remote host is not broadcast; only the ARP request for the gateway might be.",null]],["cs55",0,4,"What can a Layer 3 switch do that a Layer 2 switch cannot?",["Route traffic between VLANs or subnets","Learn MAC addresses","Forward frames within one VLAN","Provide Power over Ethernet to IP phones"],0,"A Layer 3 switch can route using IP addresses. Learning MACs and forwarding within a VLAN are basic Layer 2 functions that both perform.","Objective 4.4",[null,"Both Layer 2 and Layer 3 switches learn MAC addresses from source addresses.","Forwarding frames within a VLAN is a basic Layer 2 switch function.","Many Layer 2 switches also provide PoE; it is not tied to routing ability."]],["cs56",0,4,"Which device separates networks and forwards packets between them using IP addresses?",["Hub","Unmanaged switch","Router","Access point"],2,"Routers connect different networks and make decisions by IP address. An unmanaged switch only forwards by MAC within one network.","Objective 4.4",["A hub repeats bits out every port and has no understanding of IP addresses.","An unmanaged switch forwards frames by MAC address within one network, not between networks.",null,"An access point bridges wireless clients onto the LAN; it does not route between networks by IP."]],["cs57",0,4,"How does a switch learn which port a device is connected to?",["It asks the DNS server","It reads the source MAC address of frames arriving on each port","It reads the destination IP address of each packet","The administrator types every entry in manually"],1,"Switches record the source MAC and incoming port of each frame in the MAC address table. Destination IPs are used by routers, not for MAC learning.","Objective 4.5",["DNS maps names to IP addresses; switches never ask DNS about port locations.",null,"Layer 2 switches do not read IP addresses, and learning uses the source address, not the destination.","Static entries are possible but rarely used; switches learn entries automatically."]],["cs58",0,4,"A switch receives a frame for a MAC address that is not in its MAC address table. What does it do?",["Drops the frame","Sends it back to the sender","Sends it only to the default gateway","Floods it out all ports in the VLAN except the one it arrived on"],3,"Unknown unicast frames are flooded within the VLAN so the destination can receive them. Dropping it would break new conversations.","Objective 4.5",["Dropping unknown unicast frames would break every new conversation before the MAC was learned.","A switch never sends a frame back out the port it came in on.","A Layer 2 switch has no concept of a default gateway for forwarding frames.",null]],["cs59",0,4,"An office wants guest devices and staff PCs on the same switches but in separate broadcast domains. What should be configured?",["VLANs","A larger DHCP scope","Faster uplinks","A new SSID only"],0,"VLANs split one physical switch into separate broadcast domains. A larger DHCP scope only adds addresses, it does not separate traffic.","Objective 4.5",[null,"A larger DHCP scope only provides more addresses; all devices would still share one broadcast domain.","Faster uplinks add bandwidth but do not separate traffic.","An SSID alone does not separate wired traffic, and without VLANs guest and staff would still share one network."]],["cs60",0,4,"Two PCs are on the same switch but in different VLANs. What is needed for them to communicate?",["A longer patch cable","A router or Layer 3 switch","A hub between them","The same MAC address on both"],1,"Each VLAN is its own network, so traffic between them must be routed. A hub cannot join VLANs.","Objective 4.5",["Cable length has no effect on VLAN separation.",null,"A hub operates at Layer 1 and cannot move traffic between VLANs.","Duplicate MAC addresses would cause problems and still would not join two VLANs."]],["cs61",0,4,"A switch port connects to another switch and must carry traffic for VLANs 10, 20 and 30. How should the port be set?",["As an access port in VLAN 10","As a console port","As a trunk port","As a shutdown port"],2,"Trunk ports carry several VLANs using 802.1Q tags. An access port belongs to only one VLAN.","Objective 4.5",["An access port belongs to a single VLAN, so VLANs 20 and 30 could not cross the link.","A console port is for CLI management, not for carrying user traffic between switches.",null,"A shutdown port passes no traffic at all."]],["cs62",0,4,"An engineer asks the technician to check which switch port a PC with a known MAC address is using. Which command should be run?",["show version","show mac address-table","show ip route","show running-config | include hostname"],1,"show mac address-table lists each learned MAC with its VLAN and port. show version shows software and hardware, not MAC locations.","Objective 4.5",["show version displays software version, uptime and hardware details, not MAC-to-port mappings.",null,"show ip route shows the routing table for IP networks, not where MAC addresses are connected.","This only shows the hostname line of the configuration, which has nothing to do with MAC locations."]],["cs63",0,5,"A user reports that email stopped working. What is the first step of the troubleshooting methodology?",["Identify the problem by gathering information and asking questions","Replace the user's network cable and reboot the PC to see if it helps","Reboot the mail server","Document the fix in the ticket"],0,"Troubleshooting starts by identifying the problem: symptoms, scope and recent changes. Replacing hardware first skips the diagnosis.","Objective 5.1",[null,"Replacing hardware and rebooting are fixes attempted before the problem is understood, skipping diagnosis.","Rebooting the mail server is an untested fix that could disrupt every user.","Documentation is the last step, after the problem is fixed and verified."]],["cs64",0,5,"A technician fixes a user's printer mapping. Which step should come before closing the ticket?",["Establish a theory of probable cause","Escalate to the network team","Verify full functionality and document the solution","Question the user about what changed"],2,"After a fix, verify it works for the user and document what was done. Establishing a theory happens earlier in the process.","Objective 5.1",["Establishing a theory is the second step, done before the fix, not after it.","Escalation is for problems you cannot solve; this one is already fixed.",null,"Questioning the user belongs to the first step, identifying the problem."]],["cs65",0,5,"Two tickets arrive at once: one user cannot change a desktop wallpaper, and the whole sales floor has lost internet. How should they be handled?",["In the order they were received","Whichever is easier first","Wallpaper first because it is quick","Sales floor outage first because it affects more people and the business"],3,"Priority is based on impact and urgency; a department-wide outage outranks a single-user cosmetic issue. First-come order ignores impact.","Objective 5.1",["First-come order ignores impact and urgency, leaving a whole department down behind a cosmetic request.","Picking the easier job first ignores business impact.","A quick fix for one user's cosmetic issue should not delay restoring service to a whole department.",null]],["cs66",0,5,"A help desk technician cannot fix a switch problem and escalates it. What makes the escalation most useful?",["A ticket with symptoms, affected users, tests run and results","A short message saying the network is broken","A phone call with no ticket","Asking the user to phone the network engineer directly and explain the problem"],0,"Clear notes on symptoms, scope and steps already tried stop the next tier repeating work. A vague message forces them to start over.","Objective 5.1",[null,"A vague message forces the next tier to start again from scratch.","A phone call with no ticket leaves no record for tracking or later review.","Pushing the user to explain it themselves loses technical detail and shifts help desk work onto the user."]],["cs67",0,5,"A technician wants to capture traffic in Wireshark from a laptop's wired connection. What must be selected before starting?",["A display filter","The correct network interface","The file format .txt","The router's IP address"],1,"Wireshark captures on the interface you select, so pick the wired adapter. Display filters can be applied after capture.","Objective 5.2",["A display filter only hides packets on screen and can be applied after capturing; it does not decide what is captured.",null,"Wireshark saves captures as .pcapng or .pcap, and the file format is chosen when saving, not before capturing.","The router's IP address is not required to start a capture on a local interface."]],["cs68",0,5,"After a capture, the technician wants to view only DNS traffic in Wireshark. What should be typed in the display filter bar?",["port 53 only","show dns","dns","filter=dns"],2,"The Wireshark display filter dns shows DNS packets. The other entries are not valid display filter syntax.","Objective 5.2",["port 53 only is not valid Wireshark syntax; the display filter form would be udp.port == 53 or tcp.port == 53.","show dns is not a Wireshark filter; it looks like a device CLI command.",null,"filter=dns is not valid display filter syntax; you type the protocol name itself."]],["cs69",0,5,"An engineer asks for a packet capture to review later. How should the technician share it?",["Take a screenshot of the packet list","Copy the summary line into the ticket","Export it as a CSV of source IPs","Save it as a .pcapng file and attach it"],3,"Saving the capture as .pcapng keeps every packet so the engineer can open it in Wireshark. A screenshot loses the packet details.","Objective 5.2",["A screenshot shows only the visible summary lines and loses the full packet contents the engineer needs.","A single summary line leaves out every packet's headers and payload.","A CSV of source IPs drops ports, flags, timing and payloads, so the engineer cannot analyze the traffic.",null]],["cs70",0,5,"ping 8.8.8.8 succeeds, but ping www.example.com says it could not find the host. Which area is most likely failing?",["Name resolution (DNS)","The physical cable","The default gateway setting","The Wi-Fi password"],0,"Reaching an IP address proves the path and gateway work; failing by name points to DNS. A cable or gateway fault would also break the IP ping.","Objective 5.3",[null,"A cable fault would break the ping to 8.8.8.8 as well, and that ping succeeded.","A missing or wrong gateway would stop the ping to 8.8.8.8, which is a remote address.","A wrong Wi-Fi password would prevent the connection entirely, so no ping would succeed."]],["cs71",0,5,"A user cannot reach a website. tracert shows replies from the first three hops, then only asterisks. What does this suggest?",["The user's PC has no IP address","The problem, or an ICMP-blocking device, is beyond the third hop","The DNS server is down","The Wi-Fi password is wrong"],1,"The first hops answered, so the local link and gateway work; the issue or a firewall that drops traceroute probes lies further along the path. A PC with no IP would get no replies at all.","Objective 5.3",["A PC with no IP address would get no replies at all, but the first three hops answered.",null,"tracert to an IP runs without DNS, and a DNS outage would not make hops stop replying partway along the path.","A wrong Wi-Fi password would prevent any connection, so no hops would reply."]],["cs72",0,5,"A server's website loads fine in a browser, but ping to the server times out. What is the most likely explanation?",["The server's network card is unplugged","The server's DNS record is missing","A firewall is blocking ICMP echo requests","The server is powered off"],2,"Firewalls often block ICMP while allowing web traffic, so a failed ping does not mean the service is down. An unplugged or powered-off server would not serve the page.","Objective 5.3",["An unplugged network card would stop the website from loading too.","A missing DNS record would stop the browser from finding the site by name, but the site loads fine.",null,"A powered-off server could not serve the web page."]],["cs73",0,5,"Which command checks whether a DNS server can resolve a host name to an IP address?",["arp -a","ipconfig /release","netstat","nslookup"],3,"nslookup queries a DNS server directly. arp -a shows the local IP-to-MAC cache and does not test DNS.","Objective 5.3",["arp -a shows the local cache of IP-to-MAC mappings and does not query DNS.","ipconfig /release drops the PC's DHCP address; it does not test name resolution.","netstat lists network connections and listening ports, not DNS lookups.",null]],["cs74",0,5,"A technician runs ping 127.0.0.1 on a PC and gets replies. What does this confirm?",["The PC's TCP/IP stack is working","The default gateway is reachable","The DNS server is responding","The internet connection is up"],0,"127.0.0.1 is the loopback address, so the reply shows only that the local IP stack works. It never leaves the PC, so it says nothing about the gateway.","Objective 5.3",[null,"Loopback traffic never leaves the PC, so it says nothing about reaching the gateway.","Pinging 127.0.0.1 does not contact the DNS server at all.","Loopback replies only prove the local stack works, not that the internet link is up."]],["cs75",0,5,"An engineer needs to manage a switch remotely with an encrypted command-line session. Which protocol should be used?",["Telnet","SSH","RDP","TFTP"],1,"SSH provides an encrypted CLI session on TCP 22. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","Objective 5.4",["Telnet provides CLI access but sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.",null,"RDP is for graphical Windows desktops, not switch command-line management.","TFTP transfers files such as configs and images and is not an interactive session."]],["cs76",0,5,"A technician needs to see a Windows server's desktop from home to change a setting. Which tool fits?",["Serial console cable with PuTTY","SSH","Remote Desktop Protocol","SNMP"],2,"RDP gives a remote graphical desktop on Windows. SSH provides command-line access, not the Windows desktop.","Objective 5.4",["A serial console cable needs to be physically attached to the device and gives a CLI, not a Windows desktop from home.","SSH gives a command-line session, not the graphical Windows desktop the technician needs.",null,"SNMP monitors and manages devices with polled values; it does not display a desktop."]],["cs77",0,5,"A company's switches and access points are managed from a web dashboard hosted by the vendor, with no on-site controller. What is this approach called?",["Out-of-band console management","Telnet management","A local TFTP server","Cloud-based network management"],3,"Cloud-managed platforms such as Cisco Meraki let admins configure and monitor devices from a hosted dashboard. Console management requires a physical cable.","Objective 5.4",["Out-of-band console management uses a physical console cable or separate management network, not a vendor-hosted dashboard.","Telnet is an unencrypted command-line protocol, not a hosted web dashboard.","A local TFTP server stores configs and images on-site and offers no central dashboard.",null]],["cs78",0,5,"A remote worker must reach internal file shares securely over the internet. What should they connect with first?",["A VPN","Telnet","FTP","An open guest Wi-Fi"],0,"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel into the company network. FTP sends data unencrypted and does not provide network access.","Objective 5.4",[null,"Telnet is an unencrypted remote CLI protocol and does not give secure access to file shares.","FTP sends credentials and data unencrypted and does not provide access to the internal network.","Open guest Wi-Fi is unencrypted and does not connect the user to the company network."]],["cs79",0,5,"Which Cisco command gives a short list of every interface with its IP address and up/down status?",["show running-config","show version","show ip interface brief","show cdp neighbors"],2,"show ip interface brief shows one line per interface with IP, status and protocol. show running-config shows the whole configuration and is far longer.","Objective 5.5",["show running-config prints the entire configuration, not a one-line-per-interface status summary.","show version shows software, uptime and hardware details, not per-interface IP and status.",null,"show cdp neighbors lists directly connected Cisco devices, not interface IP addresses and status."]],["cs80",0,5,"An engineer asks which Cisco devices are directly connected to the switch and on which ports. Which command answers this?",["show ip route","show cdp neighbors","show inventory","show interfaces status"],1,"CDP discovers directly connected Cisco devices, showing their names, platforms and local ports. show ip route lists routes, not neighbors.","Objective 5.5",["show ip route lists known networks and next hops, not directly connected devices by port.",null,"show inventory lists the device's own hardware parts and serial numbers, not its neighbors.","show interfaces status shows each port's state, VLAN, duplex and speed but not which device is attached."]],["cs81",0,5,"A technician is asked for the switch's IOS version and how long it has been running since the last reload. Which command shows both?",["show version","show running-config","show mac address-table","show ip interface brief"],0,"show version displays the IOS version, uptime, model and more. The running config does not show uptime.","Objective 5.5",[null,"show running-config shows the current configuration but not uptime.","show mac address-table shows learned MAC addresses and ports, not version or uptime.","show ip interface brief summarizes interfaces and IPs, not the IOS version or uptime."]],["cs82",0,5,"The switch prompt shows Switch>. What must be typed to reach privileged EXEC mode to run more show commands?",["configure terminal","exit","show privilege","enable"],3,"enable moves from user EXEC (>) to privileged EXEC (#). configure terminal only works after you are already in privileged mode.","Objective 5.5",["configure terminal enters global configuration mode and only works from privileged EXEC, not from the > prompt.","exit leaves the current session or mode; it does not raise privilege.","show privilege displays the current privilege level but does not change it.",null]],["cs83",0,5,"A technician forgets the exact show command syntax on a Cisco switch. What is the quickest built-in help?",["Reload the switch","Type show ? to list the options","Type help show all","Unplug the console cable"],1,"Typing ? lists the valid options at that point in the command, and Tab completes partial words. help show all is not IOS syntax.","Objective 5.5",["Reloading the switch interrupts the network and gives no help with command syntax.",null,"help show all is not valid IOS syntax; IOS uses ? for context-sensitive help.","Unplugging the console cable ends the session and gives no help."]],["cs84",0,5,"show interfaces on a port shows a steadily increasing count of CRC errors. What does this most likely indicate?",["Wrong VLAN assignment","A DNS misconfiguration","A cabling or physical-layer problem","The port has no IP address"],2,"CRC errors mean frames arrive corrupted, usually from bad cables, connectors or interference. DNS and VLAN settings do not corrupt frames.","Objective 5.5",["A wrong VLAN stops traffic from reaching the right network but does not corrupt frames or cause CRC errors.","DNS problems affect name resolution above Layer 3 and cannot corrupt frames on a link.",null,"Switch ports normally have no IP address, and missing IP settings do not cause frame checksum errors."]],["cs85",0,6,"A firewall rule list has no rule matching a new incoming connection. What happens to the traffic?",["It is allowed by default","It is logged and forwarded","It is sent to the DNS server","It is dropped by the implicit deny"],3,"Firewalls end with an implicit deny, so unmatched traffic is blocked. Allowing unmatched traffic would defeat the rule list.","Objective 6.1",["Firewalls default to blocking unmatched traffic; allowing it by default would defeat the rule list.","Unmatched traffic is dropped; it may be logged, but it is not forwarded.","Firewalls do not send unmatched traffic to the DNS server.",null]],["cs86",0,6,"A small office firewall lets users browse the web, and reply traffic comes back in automatically without an inbound rule. What feature allows this?",["Stateful inspection","MAC filtering","Port mirroring","DHCP snooping on the LAN"],0,"A stateful firewall tracks outbound sessions and allows the matching return traffic. MAC filtering controls which devices can join, not return traffic.","Objective 6.1",[null,"MAC filtering controls which devices may connect; it does not track sessions to allow return traffic.","Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring port for analysis and does not permit traffic.","DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers on a switch; it does not manage return traffic through a firewall."]],["cs87",0,6,"An administrator wants to stop anyone outside the network from using Telnet to reach internal devices. Which firewall rule fits?",["Permit TCP 23 from any","Deny TCP 23 from outside","Deny UDP 53 from outside","Permit TCP 443 from any"],1,"Telnet uses TCP 23, so denying it inbound blocks outside Telnet access. UDP 53 is DNS.","Objective 6.1",["Permitting TCP 23 from any would allow outside Telnet access, the opposite of the goal.",null,"UDP 53 is DNS; blocking it would break name resolution and would not stop Telnet.","TCP 443 is HTTPS; permitting it does nothing to block Telnet."]],["cs88",0,6,"An attacker changes the amounts in a bank transfer while it is in transit. Which part of the CIA triad is affected?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Accountability"],2,"Unauthorized changes to data hurt integrity. Confidentiality is about data being read by the wrong people.","Objective 6.2",["Confidentiality is about data being seen by unauthorized people, but here the data was changed.","Availability is about systems and data being reachable when needed, not about data being altered.",null,"Accountability (tracing actions to users) is not part of the CIA triad and does not describe altered data."]],["cs89",0,6,"A web server has an unpatched flaw that attackers could use. What is the unpatched flaw called?",["A security threat","A risk","An exploit","A vulnerability"],3,"A vulnerability is a weakness. The exploit is the code or method that uses it, and the threat is who or what might use it.","Objective 6.2",["A threat is the person or event that might take advantage of a weakness, not the weakness itself.","Risk is the likelihood and impact of a threat using a weakness, not the flaw itself.","An exploit is the code or technique that takes advantage of the flaw, not the flaw.",null]],["cs90",0,6,"Thousands of infected computers flood a company's website with traffic until it stops responding. What type of attack is this?",["DDoS","Phishing","Man-in-the-middle","Tailgating"],0,"A distributed denial-of-service attack uses many systems to overwhelm a target and harms availability. Phishing tricks users with fake messages.","Objective 6.2",[null,"Phishing uses fake messages to trick users into giving up credentials, not traffic floods.","A man-in-the-middle attack intercepts or alters traffic between two parties rather than overwhelming a server.","Tailgating is a physical attack where someone follows an authorized person through a secure door."]],["cs91",0,6,"An employee gets an email that looks like it is from IT, asking them to click a link and re-enter their password. What is this?",["Ransomware","Phishing","A DoS attack","A brute-force attack"],1,"Phishing uses fake messages to steal credentials or install malware. Brute force guesses passwords rather than asking for them.","Objective 6.2",["Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; this email is trying to collect a password.",null,"A DoS attack floods a system to make it unavailable, not to trick a user.","Brute force guesses passwords automatically instead of asking the user to type one in."]],["cs92",0,6,"Malware encrypts a user's files and displays a demand for payment to unlock them. What type of malware is this?",["A keylogger","Adware","Ransomware","A rootkit"],2,"Ransomware encrypts data and demands payment. A keylogger records keystrokes but does not lock files.","Objective 6.2",["A keylogger secretly records keystrokes, but it does not encrypt files or demand payment.","Adware shows unwanted ads; it does not lock files for ransom.",null,"A rootkit hides deep in the system to keep hidden access, but it does not typically encrypt files for payment."]],["cs93",0,6,"A customer is setting up a new home router. Which wireless security option should the technician recommend?",["WPA3-Personal","WEP","Open with a hidden SSID","WPA with TKIP"],0,"WPA3-Personal is the current, strongest option for homes. WEP and TKIP are broken, and hiding an SSID provides no real security.","Objective 6.3",[null,"WEP is broken and can be cracked in minutes, so it should never be used.","Open Wi-Fi sends traffic unencrypted, and a hidden SSID is easily discovered from client probes.","WPA with TKIP is deprecated and has known weaknesses, so it is not a recommended option."]],["cs94",0,6,"A home router offers a push-button setting for connecting devices without typing the passphrase. Why should it usually be turned off?",["It forces clients to use 6 GHz","It lowers Wi-Fi range","It changes the SSID and channel every day automatically","WPS can be attacked to reveal the network key"],3,"Wi-Fi Protected Setup, especially the PIN method, has known weaknesses that can leak the passphrase. It has no effect on range or band.","Objective 6.3",["WPS has nothing to do with which band clients use.","WPS does not affect the radio's transmit power or range.","WPS does not rotate the SSID or channel; those remain whatever the router is set to.",null]],["cs95",0,6,"What is the main difference between WPA2-Personal and WPA2-Enterprise?",["Personal uses AES and Enterprise uses WEP","Enterprise authenticates each user through 802.1X, Personal uses one shared passphrase","Personal works only on 5 GHz","Enterprise needs no passwords at all"],1,"Enterprise mode checks individual credentials against an authentication server with 802.1X, while Personal uses a pre-shared key. Both use AES, not WEP.","Objective 6.3",["Both modes normally use AES; WEP is a separate, obsolete protocol, not part of WPA2-Enterprise.",null,"WPA2-Personal works on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.","Enterprise uses per-user credentials such as usernames and passwords or certificates, so it is not password-free by design."]],["cs96",0,6,"After enabling WPA3 on a home router, an older printer can no longer join. What is a reasonable fix that keeps security strong?",["Switch the router to WEP","Turn off Wi-Fi security completely","Use WPA2/WPA3 transition mode or connect the printer by cable","Share the router admin password with the printer"],2,"Transition mode lets older WPA2 devices join while newer ones use WPA3, and a cable avoids Wi-Fi entirely. WEP or open Wi-Fi would expose the whole network.","Objective 6.3",["WEP is broken and would expose every device on the network.","Turning security off lets anyone join and read traffic.",null,"The router admin password controls the router's settings; a printer does not use it and sharing it weakens security."]]]);
